 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [4], checkpoint kinase 2 [6] and tumor protein p53 [7], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [5]. \n\nComponent tumors include soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinomas [1]. \n\nAdditional Genes and Genetic Heterogeneity\n\nClinical variability is well recognized, and phenotypic heterogeneity occurs even among families carrying the same pathogenic TP53 variant [2]. Polymorphic variants in TP53-related genes, copy number variations, and epigenetic deregulation may all contribute to this clinical spectrum [2]. \n\nClinical Trial and Literature Evidence\n\nMedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [3]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [5]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [4]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [6]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [7]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [8]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [12]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [14]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [16]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [9]. Medgen clinical_features: MedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [3]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [10]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [11]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [13]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [15]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [17]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [18]. "
